{:edb/db-before-t 857 :edb/db-after-t 858 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534170 50 #inst "2026-10-06T07:10:59.944Z" 13194139534170 true] [17592186119619 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2-16}$." 13194139534170 false] [17592186119619 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2-16}.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186119619 1145 "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{1}{x^{2} - 16} \\\\\n&= (1)/((x^{2} - 4^{2})) \\\\\n&= (1)/((x + 4)(x - 4))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x+4)(x-4) = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=\\pm 4\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=4$ and $x=-4$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)$." 13194139534170 false] [17592186119619 1145 "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-{4}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x+4)(x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x+4)(x-4) = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=\\pm 4.\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=4$ and $x=-4$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186119620 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y = \\dfrac{x^2+x-6}{x^2-5x+6}$." 13194139534170 false] [17592186119620 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y = \\dfrac{x^2+x-6}{x^2-5x+6}.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186119620 1145 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} + x - 6}{x^{2} - 5x + 6} &= ((x - 2)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x + 3}{x - 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-3 = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=3\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=3$." 13194139534170 false] [17592186119620 1145 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-5x+6} & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+3)}{(x-2)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+3)}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-3 = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=3.\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=3.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186119621 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2+x}{2x+2}$." 13194139534170 false] [17592186119621 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2+x}{2x+2}.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186119621 1145 "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} + x}{2x + 2} &= (x(x + 1))/(2(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= (x(x + 1))/(2(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1815/Source/Images/b88f58b484a9741d1c21581309c5a6c4.png)" 13194139534170 false] [17592186119621 1145 "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x}{2x+2} & =\\frac{x(x+1)}{2(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cancel{(x+1)}}{2\\cancel{(x+1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/eda5457edafe14fb51fcc75ca75677b199bfc3e0aef8940c66f8aa78cf8cd5c3.png)" 13194139534170 true] [17592186119623 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}+\\dfrac{1}{x+1}$." 13194139534170 false] [17592186119623 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}+\\dfrac{1}{x+1}.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186119623 1145 "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{x + 1} &= ((x + 1))/(x(x + 1)) + (x)/(x(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= (x + 1 + x)/(x(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= (2x + 1)/(x(x + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\nx(x+1) = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=0,-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=0$ and $x=-1$." 13194139534170 false] [17592186119623 1145 "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{x+1} & =\\frac{(x+1)}{x(x+1)}+\\frac{x}{x(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1+x}{x(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\nx(x+1) = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=0,-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=0$ and $x=-1.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186119875 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280168 13194139534170 true] [17592186119875 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280173 13194139534170 true] [17592186119875 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280179 13194139534170 true] [17592186119875 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280202 13194139534170 true] [17592186119875 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280209 13194139534170 true] [17592186119876 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280184 13194139534170 true] [17592186119876 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280187 13194139534170 true] [17592186119876 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280191 13194139534170 true] [17592186119876 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280195 13194139534170 true] [17592186119877 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280133 13194139534170 true] [17592186119877 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280140 13194139534170 true] [17592186119877 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280216 13194139534170 true] [17592186119877 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280223 13194139534170 true] [17592186119878 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280147 13194139534170 true] [17592186119878 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280154 13194139534170 true] [17592186119878 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280158 13194139534170 true] [17592186119878 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280161 13194139534170 true] [17592186119878 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280230 13194139534170 true] [17592186120336 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534170 true] [17592186120336 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{2x - 6}{x^{2} - 7x + 12}$." 13194139534170 false] [17592186120336 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x-6}{{x}^{2}-7x+12}.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186120336 1145 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-6}{{x}^{2}-7x+12} & =\\frac{2x-6}{{x}^{2}-4x-3x+12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-3)}{x(x-4)-3(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-3)}{(x-4)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{(x-3)}}{(x-4)\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4.\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=4.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186120405 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534170 true] [17592186120405 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = ((x - 1)(x^{3} + 1))/(x^{2} - 1)$." 13194139534170 false] [17592186120405 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{(x-1)({x}^{3}+1)}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186120405 1145 "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)({x}^{3}+1)}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)({x}^{2}-x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}({x}^{2}-x+1)}{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=\\pm 1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/c76cce145059193c5c5348f2de347ac470f35e8b5392e196ac8b9766382bf2b8.png)" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280133 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280134 13194139534170 true] [17592186280133 1089 "q-107032" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280133 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534170 true] [17592186280133 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x}{4x-12}.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280133 1113 #uuid "93131c03-4409-5728-ae70-037a4d62a977" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280133 1145 "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x}{4x-12} & =\\frac{3x(x-3)}{4(x-3)}=\\frac{3x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/d1f0db6bba6e5bbd0079ae06a9f5199eaf8f24c4dfe0890ee691e2a5afcafc30.png)" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280135 13194139534170 true] [17592186280134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280136 13194139534170 true] [17592186280134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280137 13194139534170 true] [17592186280134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280138 13194139534170 true] [17592186280134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280139 13194139534170 true] [17592186280134 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534170 true] [17592186280134 1041 "selection" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280134 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280135 13194139534170 true] [17592186280134 1110 #uuid "7a3b7d8e-efdc-5f74-9437-1074f22c782b" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280135 1034 "No vertical asymptotes" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280135 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534170 true] [17592186280135 1111 #uuid "bc8f2183-c2ce-5e5c-ba85-e1142b6e8cd7" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280136 1034 "x=-\\frac{3}{4}" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280136 1111 #uuid "f4617fa1-f49e-5ee3-9701-3c921ef021c5" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280137 1034 "x=3" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280137 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280137 1111 #uuid "9f1927f8-eba4-5e53-8942-a620442e7454" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280138 1034 "x=-3" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280138 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280138 1111 #uuid "32d0bb0c-dd8a-56a1-8e0e-294257c95301" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280139 1034 "x=\\frac{3}{4}" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280139 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280139 1111 #uuid "266e6b07-02d9-5aab-98cc-886a115be5b2" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280140 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280141 13194139534170 true] [17592186280140 1089 "q-107068" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280140 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534170 true] [17592186280140 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{(x+4)({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-5x)}{{x}^{2}+4x}.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280140 1113 #uuid "b6e307a2-79a3-5f77-9f0e-1598db1ed452" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280140 1145 "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-5x)}{{x}^{2}+4x} & =\\frac{x(x+4)({x}^{2}+3x-5)}{x(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x(x+4)}({x}^{2}+3x-5)}{\\cancel{x(x+4)}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-4$ and $x=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/a10cef51ad5955f6065fbf4deaccbab1cda1f576db96310236ea976bb8d8eb8b.png)" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280142 13194139534170 true] [17592186280141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280143 13194139534170 true] [17592186280141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280144 13194139534170 true] [17592186280141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280145 13194139534170 true] [17592186280141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280146 13194139534170 true] [17592186280141 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534170 true] [17592186280141 1041 "selection" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280141 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280142 13194139534170 true] [17592186280141 1110 #uuid "0c71ed43-ea17-58b5-900a-ee95d6304c05" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280142 1034 "No vertical asymptotes" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280142 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534170 true] [17592186280142 1111 #uuid "b957a2dd-d89d-54fd-9fd9-8d48982962a7" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280143 1034 "x=-4,0" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280143 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280143 1111 #uuid "454847af-3c11-5d46-95e0-39ff849fe44c" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280144 1034 "x=0" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280144 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280144 1111 #uuid "dc146a87-85e8-5792-a760-ffc79dad269d" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280145 1034 "x=0,2" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280145 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280145 1111 #uuid "17bc5a6b-2555-5470-8d6c-b10ea6a7a4b3" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280146 1034 "x=-4" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280146 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280146 1111 #uuid "ae870a6e-3fa5-5a61-9bb8-42d9d952ccbb" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280147 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280148 13194139534170 true] [17592186280147 1089 "q-107074" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280147 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534170 true] [17592186280147 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1}.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280147 1113 #uuid "248881bd-12e3-5bbe-9742-1e786c69f660" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280147 1145 "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{(2{x}^{2}+1)-({x}^{2}+3)}{({x}^{2}+3)(2{x}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{{x}^{2}-2}{({x}^{2}+3)(2{x}^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+3)(2{x}^{2}+1)=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation has no real solutions, therefore $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." 13194139534170 true] [17592186280148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280149 13194139534170 true] [17592186280148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280150 13194139534170 true] [17592186280148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280151 13194139534170 true] [17592186280148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280152 13194139534170 true] [17592186280148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280153 13194139534170 true] [17592186280148 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534170 true] [17592186280148 1041 "selection" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280148 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280149 13194139534170 true] [17592186280148 1110 #uuid "91349b59-7134-5667-a560-f319c0e88525" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280149 1034 "No vertical asymptotes" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280149 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534170 true] [17592186280149 1111 #uuid "10ed4443-3771-50f6-b284-f540a89ebaa6" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280150 1034 "x=\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280150 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280150 1111 #uuid "2c6214c7-c047-5571-918f-806e66367cbb" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280151 1034 "$x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280151 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534170 true] [17592186280151 1111 #uuid "1b180fba-b286-597c-848a-ecd7a6c8bb1d" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280152 1034 "x=\\sqrt{2}" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280152 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280152 1111 #uuid "5585a7ca-2dc5-5f58-87ba-bfd458b1c17c" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280153 1034 "$x=\\sqrt{3}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280153 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534170 true] [17592186280153 1111 #uuid "557ee11b-1196-5a8d-a454-92f0b8a444d5" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280154 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280155 13194139534170 true] [17592186280154 1089 "q-339757" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280154 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534170 true] [17592186280154 1100 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{7}{4x+12}+\\frac{5}{x+3}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534170 true] [17592186280154 1113 #uuid "37ba1c34-45da-5976-b370-8f8ed7d7f89d" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280154 1145 "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4x+12}+\\frac{5}{x+3} & =\\frac{7}{4(x+3)}+\\frac{5}{x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7+5(4)}{4(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4(x+3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n4(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is at $x=-3.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280156 13194139534170 true] [17592186280155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280157 13194139534170 true] [17592186280155 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534170 true] [17592186280155 1041 "field-1" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280155 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280157 13194139534170 true] [17592186280155 1110 #uuid "e3851eaa-4b73-5340-8c90-12d044fed340" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280156 1034 "3" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280156 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280156 1111 #uuid "fe466a23-c6fe-5259-be76-7706806a4b6e" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280157 1034 "-3" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280157 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280157 1111 #uuid "de50e952-8725-53a0-8b0d-f0bc581fe47d" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280158 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280159 13194139534170 true] [17592186280158 1089 "q-339763" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280158 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534170 true] [17592186280158 1100 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{9}{4x-3}-\\frac{3}{8x-6}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534170 true] [17592186280158 1113 #uuid "a2085814-78d7-55fd-bc67-38e63d4c9599" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280158 1145 "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4x-3}-\\frac{3}{8x-6} & =\\frac{9}{4x-3}-\\frac{3}{2(4x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(2)-3}{2(4x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2(4x-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n2(4x-3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is at $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280160 13194139534170 true] [17592186280159 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534170 true] [17592186280159 1041 "field-1" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280159 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280160 13194139534170 true] [17592186280159 1110 #uuid "c062f8f2-f8e5-54ab-8ca9-047e3f359d3c" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280160 1034 "\\frac{3}{4}" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280160 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280160 1111 #uuid "c69b8c7d-78cb-50c6-a3f0-3c7b16369b0e" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280161 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280162 13194139534170 true] [17592186280161 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280164 13194139534170 true] [17592186280161 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280166 13194139534170 true] [17592186280161 1089 "q-339779" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280161 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534170 true] [17592186280161 1100 "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-9}-\\frac{1}{x+5}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,$\n and $\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139534170 true] [17592186280161 1113 #uuid "fedc34c5-d7a5-5801-9c83-0ce6f45aaef0" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280161 1145 "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-9}-\\frac{1}{x+5} & =\\frac{x(x+5)-({x}^{2}-9)}{({x}^{2}-9)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-{x}^{2}+9}{({x}^{2}-9)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x+9}{({x}^{2}-9)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x+9}{(x-3)(x+3)(x+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x+3)(x+5)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-5\\,,\\,x=-3\\,,\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=-5$,  $x=-3,$ and $x=3.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280162 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280163 13194139534170 true] [17592186280162 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534170 true] [17592186280162 1041 "field-1" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280162 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280163 13194139534170 true] [17592186280162 1110 #uuid "fc87fae7-f9f2-599a-af68-66769c52ea50" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280163 1034 "-5" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280163 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280163 1111 #uuid "cade2cb1-3a31-5320-a2d0-529fb4a4001c" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280165 13194139534170 true] [17592186280164 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534170 true] [17592186280164 1041 "field-2" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280164 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280165 13194139534170 true] [17592186280164 1110 #uuid "5dbf238b-7e20-5377-9a4e-a28bf608f9ef" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280165 1034 "-3" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280165 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280165 1111 #uuid "64095e03-4823-54bd-846a-918e3528bd4d" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280167 13194139534170 true] [17592186280166 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534170 true] [17592186280166 1041 "field-3" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280166 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280167 13194139534170 true] [17592186280166 1110 #