right, the vertical asymptotes are $x=-3$ and $x=7,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/56b3d93afca64c37cd48013702f2317f41935e85d5b795c34f31a76927852d44.png)" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280181 13194139534170 true] [17592186280180 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534170 true] [17592186280180 1041 "field-1" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280180 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280181 13194139534170 true] [17592186280180 1110 #uuid "c3f129fe-fa2b-5846-b334-35af1aebc21d" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280181 1034 "-3" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280181 1111 #uuid "44c44301-edd4-5f24-91a6-6af2cc80d3bc" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280183 13194139534170 true] [17592186280182 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534170 true] [17592186280182 1041 "field-2" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280182 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280183 13194139534170 true] [17592186280182 1110 #uuid "cdadc70f-39b7-5af5-842d-f7c154d28196" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280183 1034 "7" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280183 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280183 1111 #uuid "39b8a916-a195-5e48-b50c-510ef6cc92a4" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280184 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280185 13194139534170 true] [17592186280184 1089 "q-339960" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280184 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534170 true] [17592186280184 1100 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{4x-8}{2{x}^{2}+2x-12}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534170 true] [17592186280184 1113 #uuid "399f0d67-1eb9-57db-a89e-719174076197" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280184 1145 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x-8}{2{x}^{2}+2x-12} & =\\frac{4(x-2)}{2(x+3)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{(x-2)}}{2(x+3)\\cancel{(x-2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+3=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-3.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280185 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280186 13194139534170 true] [17592186280185 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534170 true] [17592186280185 1041 "field-1" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280185 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280186 13194139534170 true] [17592186280185 1110 #uuid "b7c6390b-6c28-57b3-a1b9-b88e20a4f1aa" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280186 1034 "-3" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280186 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280186 1111 #uuid "a5660f26-e5fd-5046-a763-c39d7e6a2f22" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280187 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280188 13194139534170 true] [17592186280187 1089 "q-339971" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280187 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534170 true] [17592186280187 1100 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x}{{x}^{2}-4x}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534170 true] [17592186280187 1113 #uuid "469b9897-9d0c-516a-93de-61de2ed705a3" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280187 1145 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x}{{x}^{2}-4x} & =\\frac{2x(x+4)}{x(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{x}(x+4)}{\\cancel{x}(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+4)}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=4.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280188 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280189 13194139534170 true] [17592186280188 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280190 13194139534170 true] [17592186280188 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534170 true] [17592186280188 1041 "field-1" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280188 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280189 13194139534170 true] [17592186280188 1110 #uuid "efe1a369-1e4d-591d-9265-7d6eb297aa60" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280189 1034 "4" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280189 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280189 1111 #uuid "20d3780f-cd43-5899-9334-a4e849304d08" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280190 1034 "0" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280190 1111 #uuid "a4d29871-5f53-514f-86eb-12c448d859ee" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280191 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280192 13194139534170 true] [17592186280191 1089 "q-339976" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280191 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534170 true] [17592186280191 1100 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-8}{{x}^{2}+7x+10}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534170 true] [17592186280191 1113 #uuid "ff284a84-3518-5c5c-aee6-4a46c4366526" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280191 1145 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-8}{{x}^{2}+7x+10} & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-4)}{(x+5)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{2}^{2})}{(x+5)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-2)(x+2)}{(x+5)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-2)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{(x+5)\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-2)}{x+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+5=0\\,⟹\\,x=-5\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-5.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280193 13194139534170 true] [17592186280192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280194 13194139534170 true] [17592186280192 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534170 true] [17592186280192 1041 "field-1" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280192 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280193 13194139534170 true] [17592186280192 1110 #uuid "b7136970-f63a-5d20-948b-d530596f642f" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280193 1034 "-5" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280193 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280193 1111 #uuid "9643f39a-af86-5403-bd4b-40bed5569f7e" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280194 1034 "-2" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280194 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280194 1111 #uuid "ff7869e3-8f98-5186-962f-b4b9d4d83767" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280195 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280196 13194139534170 true] [17592186280195 1089 "q-37069" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280195 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534170 true] [17592186280195 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{12x}{{x}^{2}-4x}.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280195 1113 #uuid "3c3564f2-172b-5ce6-973c-55bb62901f2e" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280195 1145 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12x}{{x}^{2}-4x} & =\\frac{12x}{x(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4.\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=4.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280197 13194139534170 true] [17592186280196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280198 13194139534170 true] [17592186280196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280199 13194139534170 true] [17592186280196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280200 13194139534170 true] [17592186280196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280201 13194139534170 true] [17592186280196 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534170 true] [17592186280196 1041 "selection" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280196 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280199 13194139534170 true] [17592186280196 1110 #uuid "ec21cfe9-7452-5681-9fd2-68af08dcd5d3" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280197 1034 "No vertical asymptotes" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280197 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534170 true] [17592186280197 1111 #uuid "44faa13f-ac1d-5d1b-9aa6-fc59240d2e3b" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280198 1034 "$x=0$ and $x=4$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280198 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534170 true] [17592186280198 1111 #uuid "649bcf2b-3996-5f0e-8b86-ac96581c94c2" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280199 1034 "x=4" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280199 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280199 1111 #uuid "5357c30f-ca28-5534-9542-a5f0c2885800" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280200 1034 "x=0" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280200 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280200 1111 #uuid "49a04468-d369-5d0f-9938-37d171867b02" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280201 1034 "x=12" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280201 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280201 1111 #uuid "50d19829-9542-5ff1-8ca3-3e2e1414532a" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280202 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280203 13194139534170 true] [17592186280202 1089 "q-47853" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280202 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534170 true] [17592186280202 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x-6}.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280202 1113 #uuid "53858ba9-755e-54c6-83a7-797ed7120dc6" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280202 1145 "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-6)(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-6)(x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1,6.\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=6$ and $x=-1$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/b3d4116e1157d2f9b5414a015965b371982663518d04631810f19d8be4a7de93.png)" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280204 13194139534170 true] [17592186280203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280205 13194139534170 true] [17592186280203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280206 13194139534170 true] [17592186280203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280207 13194139534170 true] [17592186280203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280208 13194139534170 true] [17592186280203 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534170 true] [17592186280203 1041 "selection" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280203 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280205 13194139534170 true] [17592186280203 1110 #uuid "f28c8f37-1cb0-51be-b67f-af017d0511c9" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280204 1034 "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280204 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534170 true] [17592186280204 1111 #uuid "85b5a260-ac60-52ea-a55c-34f8f616a856" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280205 1034 "$x=-1$ and $x=6$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280205 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534170 true] [17592186280205 1111 #uuid "155cbef5-d4bf-5d19-9119-2b6c4f607dac" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280206 1034 "$x=1$ and $x=-6$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280206 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534170 true] [17592186280206 1111 #uuid "cbc32ec7-8a46-508f-87ef-c55ea86bd785" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280207 1034 "There are no vertical asymptotes." 13194139534170 true] [17592186280207 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534170 true] [17592186280207 1111 #uuid "370f7a98-302d-5a74-8f4d-c2d5a1d408df" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280208 1034 "$x=3$ and $x=-2$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280208 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534170 true] [17592186280208 1111 #uuid "92fffbe3-63fa-51f0-954e-96df25de2048" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280209 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280210 13194139534170 true] [17592186280209 1089 "q-47854" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280209 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534170 true] [17592186280209 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\frac{5}{x-\\sqrt{2}}.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280209 1113 #uuid "b2c4ac97-8cea-5552-8956-b9b9e4733346" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280209 1145 "There are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-\\sqrt{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is the only vertical asymptote of $y=f(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/09c19a6049d77a2b57c093ca714627a453c99bf52af27ae656ad1f4e779d1866.png)" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280211 13194139534170 true] [17592186280210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280212 13194139534170 true] [17592186280210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280213 13194139534170 true] [17592186280210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280214 13194139534170 true] [17592186280210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280215 13194139534170 true] [17592186280210 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534170 true] [17592186280210 1041 "selection" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280210 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280211 13194139534170 true] [17592186280210 1110 #uuid "4018887a-b7dc-5e26-88f3-652e88837dce" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280211 1034 "x=\\sqrt{2}" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280211 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280211 1111 #uuid "96b3dec0-1f05-5c68-9512-bdebc2a567fa" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280212 1034 "x=2" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280212 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280212 1111 #uuid "98948942-15fd-517d-810b-d0c9bd4b0be8" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280213 1034 "x=5" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280213 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280213 1111 #uuid "de41d696-daab-5b8e-b788-fc554e5810f0" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280214 1034 "There are no vertical asymptotes." 13194139534170 true] [17592186280214 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534170 true] [17592186280214 1111 #uuid "b655f1ec-64ce-56c1-83c3-6c678e031c46" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280215 1034 "x=-2" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280215 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280215 1111 #uuid "d50dd59c-e9e5-56f4-a0fb-4e4cfb60de89" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280216 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280217 13194139534170 true] [17592186280216 1089 "q-68151" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280216 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534170 true] [17592186280216 1100 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6x+3}.$" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280216 1113 #uuid "2a320ace-3bfe-55b8-b3d5-e7e54548d7c4" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280216 1145 "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6x+3} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+1)}{3(2x+1)}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/7ca0a626369d11d7d3cd8e2d99cbfb79eb37c534b86a1df3f43aaeff5fb932e3.png)" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280218 13194139534170 true] [17592186280217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280219 13194139534170 true] [17592186280217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280220 13194139534170 true] [17592186280217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280221 13194139534170 true] [17592186280217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280222 13194139534170 true] [17592186280217 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534170 true] [17592186280217 1041 "selection" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280217 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280218 13194139534170 true] [17592186280217 1110 #uuid "a526f5cc-91f2-57ef-928c-83ed219915f0" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280218 1034 "No vertical asymptotes" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280218 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534170 true] [17592186280218 1111 #uuid "d1674388-12a5-5e6a-a202-8505f299e592" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280219 1034 "x=-\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280219 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280219 1111 #uuid "e3580144-80cd-57e6-8cab-a52ad9fcb289" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280220 1034 "x=\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139534170 true] [17592186280220 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534170 true] [17592186280220 1111 #uuid "4d037fec-3ae7-5727-b1c3-1e4cb1ec